Stop a release tarball reporting its version as "22.1.8None".

utils/bazel/configure.bzl parses llvm/CMakeLists.txt for the version pieces
and seeds each one with None. A release tarball never says

    set(LLVM_VERSION_SUFFIX ...)

because the suffix is what marks a development tree, so that entry stays
None and PACKAGE_VERSION -- which is what clang --version prints -- comes out
as "22.1.8None".

The fix cannot be to seed the dict with "" instead: the parser uses
`if c[k] != None: continue` to mean "already found", so a non-None seed would
make it ignore a suffix that is genuinely set. Normalising after the parse
leaves the git-checkout case alone.

--- a/utils/bazel/configure.bzl
+++ b/utils/bazel/configure.bzl
@@ -129,6 +129,14 @@
         v = kv[i:].strip().partition(")")[0].partition(" ")[0]
         c[k] = v
 
+    # A release tarball never says set(LLVM_VERSION_SUFFIX ...) -- the suffix
+    # is what marks a development tree -- so the parse above leaves it None
+    # and every string built from it below reads "22.1.8None". The parser
+    # treats a non-None value as "already found", so this cannot be the
+    # dict's initial value; it has to be normalised once parsing is done.
+    if c["LLVM_VERSION_SUFFIX"] == None:
+        c["LLVM_VERSION_SUFFIX"] = ""
+
     # Synthesize `LLVM_VERSION` for convenience.
     c["LLVM_VERSION"] = "{}.{}.{}".format(
         c["LLVM_VERSION_MAJOR"],
